Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Breastfeed: Breastfeeding may have a protective effect against postpartum depression (PPD) as it promotes bonding and hormonal benefits, but refusal or inability to breastfeed can be a stressor.  
- Feeling about motherhood: Negative feelings (Sad) or even Neutral feelings about motherhood can be indicative or predictive of PPD, while Happy feelings suggest lower risk.  
- Mode of delivery: Caesarean Section is sometimes linked with higher risk of PPD compared to Normal Delivery due to recovery and complications, but this is not deterministic.  
- Relationship with the newborn: A good or very good relationship suggests bonding and lower risk of PPD; neutral or bad relationships may indicate higher risk.  
- Pregnancy plan: Unplanned pregnancy (No) can increase stress and risk of PPD, while planned pregnancy (Yes) is generally protective.  
- Number of household members: Smaller household size (2 to 5) may indicate less social support and higher risk; larger households (6 to 8 or 9 or more) might indicate more support and lower risk.  
- Relationship between father and newborn: Good or very good relationship suggests family support and lower risk; neutral or bad may increase risk.  
- Marital status: Married status generally provides social support and reduces risk; divorced status may increase risk of PPD.  
- Occupation before latest pregnancy: Occupations with higher stress or less social support (e.g., Student, Housewife) may be associated with higher risk; professional occupations may vary.  
- Number of the latest pregnancy: First pregnancy (1) may be associated with higher anxiety and risk; multiple pregnancies (>=2) may have different risk patterns depending on experience and support.  

